Hot/cold routed-expert split for llama.cpp (glm5next, deepseek4), the runtime half of tools/moe_placement.

Base: unslothai/llama.cpp glm5next/upstream @ 86ebfef (the branch that runs GLM-5.3-Flash and DeepSeek V4 Flash).
Apply: git apply --whitespace=nowarn llama.cpp-moe-hot-cold-experts.patch   (git ignores this header)

What it does: when a GGUF made by tools/moe_placement/gguf_hotcold.py has blk.L.ffn_*_exps_hot / _cold tensors, the
layer's routed experts are computed in two parts: the hot part follows the layer's device, the cold part goes wherever
-ot puts it (e.g. `-ot 'ffn_.*_exps_cold\.=CPU'`). Routing is untouched (canonical ids, biases, hash tables); each
selected id goes through I32 lookup tables to an index in each part, the part that does not hold it uses its all-zero
expert, and hot + cold is added before the usual weighting. Original GGUFs load and run exactly as before.

CUDA backends need distinct expert ids per token (their mul_mat_id groups tokens by expert): layers placed on CUDA
are rewritten with one zero expert per routing slot (gguf_hotcold.py --slot-zeros) and a [1, n_expert, k] map; the
graph then looks ids up per slot. Run the server with --no-op-offload: op offload reads ids before the split runs.

Limits: SILU-gated experts without biases/scales/merged gate_up only (asserted); the cold part runs every layer
(its zero expert is computed even when no cold expert is selected), which costs one expert read per split layer.
The large diff in llama-graph.cpp is re-indentation of the original expert block under an else; `git diff -w` is +31.

